    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sleep is a universal human need. For adults, it helps the brain to process information and transfer short-term memory to long-term memory. For children, it not only acts as a processor but also as an enforcer of executive functions including focusing attention and emotional management in the face of obstacles. Therefore, sleep is important both for health and learning. \r\nYoung children ages 3-5 need to sleep 11-13 hours each day. In addition, the adults – the parents – need 7-9 hours of sleep each day. Routine is a mechanism that parents use to balance all of the obligations in a day to ensure the important activities including meals and sleep get done. For instance, a typical evening routine consists of dinner, bath, and bedtime story. However, two main issues make enforcing routines difficult in practice. First, children tend to resist transitions from one activity to another. Second, parents struggle to implement routines with consistency. \r\nPeerHouse is a solution that addresses the aforementioned issues. Children learn and practice following routines through modeling the behavior of a character programmed to mirror the family’s routines. The character signals transitions in the routines through auditory and visual cues to remind and gently nudge parents and children to follow their routines. The initial user tests show that children can form strong emotional bonds with the character and understand that the character has a routine. Future learner studies will assess the long-term impact of the intervention including children’s sustained interest after the novelty effect wears off, and lasting behavior change in  consistency.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
